  "question": {
    "text": "An air bubble of volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;rises from the bottom of a lake <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> deep to the surface at a temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The atmospheric pressure is &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Pa</mi><mo>,</mo></math> the density of water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo></math>There is no difference of the temperature of water at the depth of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and on the surface. The volume of air bubble when it reaches the surface will be",
